{{also|scherzò|Scherzo}}
==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|scherzo||joke, play}}, from {{m|it|scherzare||to joke, jest}}, from {{der|en|lng|*skerzan||to jump merrily, enjoy oneself, jest}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skertaną||to hop, jump}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kerǝd-||to dance, jump}}. Akin to {{cog|gmh|scherzen||to frolic, jump merrily, hop up and down}} (modern {{cog|de|scherzen||to joke}}, {{m|de|Scherz}}), {{cog|no|skjerta||to joke}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈskɛətsəʊ/|/ˈskɜːt-/|a=RP}}
* {{enPR|skĕrʹtsō(')|skĕrtʹsō(')|a=GA}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈskɛəɹ(ˌ)tsoʊ/|/ˈskɛəɹtˌsoʊ/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛəɹtsəʊ|ɜː(ɹ)tsəʊ|ɛə(ɹ)tsəʊ|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|scher|zo}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|scherzi}}

# {{lb|en|music}} A piece of music or a [[movement]] from a larger piece such as a [[symphony]]; especially, a piece of music played in a playful manner.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1980|author=Anthony Burgess|title=Earthly Powers
|passage=At seven Val knocked – three shorts and one long, out of the '''scherzo''' of Beethoven’s Fifth – and I rushed to open.}}

